<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/weathering-erosion-and-landforms">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The surface of the Earth is <strong>a demolition and redistribution project running for 4.5
    billion years</strong>: weathering breaks rock, erosion moves it, deposition files it. Read
    any landscape and you are reading which process dominated, for how long.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="break, move, sort, read" widget="equationStep" config={weatherCfg} />

  <h2>Why this matters practically</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Soil is weathering plus biology:</strong> centuries to form, years to wash away — erosion rates on conventionally-farmed land commonly exceed formation by 10–100× (the agriculture lesson\'s stakes).</li>
    <li><strong>Sediment is history:</strong> layered rocks preserve environments in order — the geologic-time lesson\'s archive exists because deposition documents everything.</li>
    <li><strong>Hazard reading:</strong> landslide scars, ancient flood deposits, coastal erosion rates — development decisions that ignore geomorphology eventually meet it.</li>
    <li><strong>The human acceleration:</strong> agriculture and construction move more sediment than all natural erosion combined (the "Anthropocene" argument in one measurement — Wilkinson\'s estimate).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Press & Siever, Understanding Earth; Wilkinson, Geology (2005) on human sediment; the geography ladder\'s rivers and coasts lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
